 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**No. The Aussie dollar is strong against the yen right now, even though it has dropped back over the last six weeks.**\n\n**Latest rate:** A$1 = **¥109.67** (RBA 4pm rate, Friday 16 October 2026).\n\n**Compared with recent history (RBA month-end rates):**\n- It's up about 20% from **¥91.43 in April 2025**, the low point since 2023. In January 2023 it was ¥91.69.\n- It's well above the 2023–24 range of roughly ¥89–107. The previous high was **¥106.61 in June 2024**.\n- It peaked at about **¥114.5** at the end of August 2026 (¥114.54) and again on 1 September 2026 (¥114.63). It also hit ¥114.28 in April 2026.\n\n**The recent drop:** Since 1 September it has fallen about 4% (¥114.63 to ¥109.67), and for the past month it has stayed between about ¥109.4 and ¥110.7. This seems to be mostly the Aussie dollar weakening generally, not just against the yen. Over the same period:\n- Against the US dollar it fell from US$0.717 to **US$0.6947**.\n- The trade-weighted index, which measures the A$ against a basket of currencies, fell from 66.2 to **64.4**.\n\n**Longer view:** My data only goes back to 2023. From memory, not from this data, A$/¥ mostly stayed below about ¥105 before then. So today's level of around ¥110 would be near the top of its range over several decades.\n\nIf you're buying yen for a trip to Japan, you'd get about ¥5 less per A$1 than in late August. That's still far more than the ¥90-odd you'd have got in 2023 or early 2025.",
